{"id":"spam-threshold-at-fpr","t":"Spam filter threshold at a false positive budget","tags":["Classification","Metrics & Evaluation"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"threshold_at_fpr","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"A spam filter must not send more than a fixed share of legitimate mail to the spam folder. Write `threshold_at_fpr(scores, labels, max_fpr)` where label 1 is spam and a message is flagged when `score >= t`.\n\n- Consider as candidate thresholds every distinct score, plus `+inf` (flag nothing).\n- Pick the **smallest** candidate whose false positive rate (flagged ham / all ham) is at most `max_fpr`.\n- Return `(t, fpr, recall)` where recall is flagged spam / all spam. If there is no ham, FPR is 0; if there is no spam, recall is 0.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef threshold_at_fpr(scores, labels, max_fpr):\n    \"\"\"Return (threshold, fpr, recall).\"\"\"\n    t = float(np.quantile(scores, 1 - max_fpr))\n    return t, max_fpr, 0.0\n","hints":["FPR only falls as the threshold rises, so scan thresholds upward and stop at the first that fits.","Ties matter: every message with the same score is flagged together, so a quantile can overshoot the budget."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef threshold_at_fpr(scores, labels, max_fpr):\n    s, y = np.asarray(scores, float), np.asarray(labels)\n    n_ham, n_spam = int((y == 0).sum()), int((y == 1).sum())\n    for t in list(np.unique(s)) + [np.inf]:\n        flag = s >= t\n        fpr = (flag & (y == 0)).sum() / n_ham if n_ham else 0.0\n        if fpr <= max_fpr:\n            rec = (flag & (y == 1)).sum() / n_spam if n_spam else 0.0\n            return float(t), float(fpr), float(rec)\n","ex_md":"## Asymmetric costs\nA spam message in the inbox is an annoyance; a job offer in the spam folder can cost someone a great deal. Spam systems therefore fix a false positive budget on legitimate mail and maximise recall within it, rather than optimising accuracy or F1.\n\n## Why the smallest threshold\nAmong thresholds that meet the budget, the lowest one flags the most spam. With tied scores you cannot split a tie, which is why the achieved FPR can sit well below the budget.\n\n## In production\nThe budget is usually set per user segment and re-checked after every model update, because a recalibrated model shifts the score distribution and an old threshold no longer gives the same FPR.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How do you estimate FPR when users rarely report mail wrongly marked as spam?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"[0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8]\n[0, 0, 1, 1]\n0.0","out":"(0.8, 0.0, 0.5)"},{"in":"[0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8]\n[0, 0, 1, 1]\n0.5","out":"(0.35, 0.5, 1.0)"}],"lessons":[],"related":["threshold-at-precision","auc-roc"],"theory":[],"ntests":6,"tj":"[{\"a\": [[0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8], [0, 0, 1, 1], 0.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [0.8, 0.0, 0.5]}}, {\"a\": [[0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8], [0, 0, 1, 1], 0.5], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [0.35, 0.5, 1.0]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.8078152065776814, 0.6825784562774218, 0.46128634334998647, 0.2556759804305101, 0.5739044836077898, 0.16152219122018857, 0.6223800805645106, 0.6816788527186247, 0.2515027830904688, 0.3836294985540719, 0.6193385447533868, 0.6385262383925101, 0.44238432928346316, 0.20509568539496548, 0.6358989920470401, 0.6357020298996086, 0.8461552342438606, 0.5077633736536321, 0.5811303560128133, 0.7586518092691316, 0.8050001635048293, 0.1939949328437461, 0.9499115621303551, 0.6962360380054952, 0.7548423615526731, 0.0, 0.4910817736264755, 0.5462923080906688, 0.5814134778097079, 0.4404888614950532, 0.7996652796665485, 0.7654763024589796, 0.2420681487830898, 0.3216124340088869, 0.8538078933561355, 0.6054640168438128, 0.669795368721606, 0.36540649580800083, 0.39009927074942813, 0.857533041968376, 0.13919230641126337, 0.0841868332102213, 0.37486299323773964, 0.12869424639929733, 0.232578105066982, 0.36725914765972706, 0.44650098608311595, 0.4355816329039459, 0.5103210893510404, 0.2952570755609726, 0.4590832098551565, 0.3344536497681021, 0.09501532156170373, 